+# Experimental RISC-V support
+
+This port adapts the RISC-V backend proposed in Firecracker PR #5227
+(commit `910b4f40b3367f720d6508fbad8a89bd968e7078`) to the 1.17 VM and device APIs.
+
+The host must provide RV64 KVM virtualization and the KVM RISC-V AIA device.
+An ordinary RISC-V machine without the hypervisor extension cannot run microVMs
+natively. The backend checks KVM capabilities and reports missing capabilities;
+it does not fall back to emulation inside Firecracker.
+
+Use an uncompressed RISC-V Linux `Image`, optionally with an initramfs, and
+`console=ttyS0` for the serial console. Guest RAM starts at `0x80000000`; the
+device tree occupies the reserved area below the kernel's `0x80200000` entry
+point. The device tree reports the KVM timer frequency, guest ISA extensions,
+memory, UART, AIA, initramfs, and VirtIO MMIO devices. Secondary harts start via
+KVM's SBI implementation.
+
+Device eventfds are serviced by the VMM event loop, which pulses each APLIC
+line with `KVM_IRQ_LINE`. RISC-V KVM's IRQFD in-atomic path asserts a wired
+line without deasserting it; connecting edge-triggered APLIC sources directly
+to IRQFD loses subsequent interrupts. Eventfds remain usable by VirtIO and
+vhost-user devices, and the VM owns the descriptors for their full lifetime.
+The device tree also supplies fresh host-generated entropy at each boot.
+
+The initial port uses VirtIO MMIO, not PCI. Snapshot save/restore, CPU register
+modifiers, CPU-configuration dumps, SMT, VMGenID, and VMClock are not implemented.
+Unsupported snapshot and CPU-configuration operations return errors rather
+than panicking. Architecture-independent KVM capability templates are accepted.
+
+The seccomp compiler recognizes the RISC-V audit architecture. As with upstream's
+other GNU-libc targets, the GNU build uses the upstream empty fallback policy
+unless an explicit seccomp filter is supplied; recognizing the architecture does
+not supply a production seccomp policy.
+
+The Rust test helpers generate actual RISC-V Image-format loop and dirty-memory
+workloads, rather than using an ARM image or a placeholder binary. Running KVM
+tests requires a compatible KVM host; merely compiling the tests does not verify
+guest execution.
